Here's how to save and restore draft text using Window lifecycle events:

**Create a custom `Window` subclass in `App.xaml.cs`:**

```csharp
public partial class App : Application
{
    protected override Window CreateWindow(IActivationState? activationState)
        => new AppWindow(new AppShell());
}

public class AppWindow : Window
{
    public AppWindow(Page page) : base(page) { }

    protected override void OnStopped()
    {
        base.OnStopped();
        SaveDraft();
    }

    protected override void OnDestroying()
    {
        base.OnDestroying();
        SaveDraft(); // Android back-button can skip Stopped
    }

    protected override void OnResumed()
    {
        base.OnResumed();
        if (Shell.Current?.CurrentPage is NotePage note)
            note.ViewModel.DraftText = Preferences.Get("draft_text", string.Empty);
    }

    private void SaveDraft()
    {
        if (Shell.Current?.CurrentPage is NotePage note)
            Preferences.Set("draft_text", note.ViewModel.DraftText);
    }
}
```

**Restore on page load too** (first launch doesn't fire `Resumed`):

```csharp
public partial class NotePage : ContentPage
{
    public NoteViewModel ViewModel { get; }

    public NotePage(NoteViewModel vm)
    {
        InitializeComponent();
        BindingContext = ViewModel = vm;
        vm.DraftText = Preferences.Get("draft_text", string.Empty);
    }
}
```

**Key points:**

- **`OnStopped`** — fires when the app is fully backgrounded; save here.
- **`OnDestroying`** — also save here because Android's back button can skip `Stopped`.
- **`OnResumed`** — fires when returning from background; restore here.
- **`Resumed` does NOT fire on first launch** — load saved state in your page constructor too.
- **Keep handlers fast** (<1–2 sec) — `Preferences` is ideal for small text. For large drafts, use file I/O off the main thread before the lifecycle event.
